# Round q38q3ref: ik's CPU node dumps of the Qwen3.8-Flash-Next UD-Q3_K_XL split set (three shards,
# 89,986,353,824 B, summed from the shards on the box), each one `just dump-ref-qwen4exp-q3
# [VARIANT]` under this card: the batch set, then step4, step4-every-node, d1k and d3k, one lease
# each, back to back. dump_ref loads without --defer-experts, so ik's loader populates every shard in
# turn (one stream), then computes on 32 threads, then writes the nodes to /root/bloomery-data; the
# three phases run one after the other and the wall is their sum, as the Q4 file's card
# (q38ref-dump.card) derives. Minutes per dump [derived], that card's 0.2..2.2 scaled by the file
# size, 89,986,353,824 / 111,334,654,784 = 0.808: 0.16..1.78, rounded outward. The scaling is the
# populate term's (it reads the whole split set cold, and it dominates the cold upper bound); the
# warm step dumps' fixed lease, witness and metadata seconds do not shrink with the file, which the
# rounded lower bound absorbs. The compute term is the Q4 card's unchanged — the same 6.6 B active
# parameters over the same token counts; this file's quant packs smaller expert stacks, which moves
# bytes read, not FLOPs. A step dump that reads the file from the device again costs the populate
# term again; all five cold is under 9 min.
kind: exclusive
reason: dump_ref runs ik on 32 CPU threads and populates the whole 90.0 GB split set into the page
  cache, evicting that much of what else is cached (the V4.1 host set or the Q4 set among it);
  beside a timed run or a gate batch it would take their cores, their memory bandwidth and their
  cache, so it holds the lease; why now: the ik-qwen4exp-q3 oracle family the Qwen3.8 program's e2e
  gate is judged against on the Q3 file (round q38q3ref)
minutes: 0.15..1.8
